At our college, the computer club maintained a discussion list 
that allowed anonymous postings.  It was called "The Wall"--perhaps 
by analogy with the Berlin wall, or a bathroom wall.  Things worked 
well for about a year, after which omebody began to post libelous 
material.  The college was afraid that it might be held liable for 
these anonymous postings, so the club closed down the discussion.  
Here's a copy of the message that closed things down.

>NOTEBOOK    The Wall is DEAD. Long live the Wall.          Tue Dec 07 
>21:28 1993
>====================================================================
>
>VTC^3 Regrets to inform the college community of the untimely demise 
>of the Wall. VTC^3 decided that we could not take the legal 
>responsibilities of maintaining an anonymous forum.
>
>HOWEVER, we are currently thinking about an alternative system that 
>would allow anonymous postings. The two choices that we are 
>considering are :
>
>(1) A Moderated Topic. This topic would have a moderator and all 
>posts would be reviewed before they were posted in the topic.
>
>(2) A topic that maintained a list of who had posted anonymously and 
>stored that information in a data file that could only be accessed 
>by the Network SUPERVISOR. This file would NOT be availible to 
>anyone OTHER THAN the SUPERVISOR. This file would also NOT be used 
>by the SUPERVISOR at any time other than legal cases where the 
>college could be considered liable for the contents.
>
>VTC^3 would like to solicit your opinions on this matter. Comments 
>may be posted here in this topic or they may be posted via EMAIL to 
>the user NOTEBOOK.
>
>Thank you for your understanding in this matter.

I would like to hear people's reactions to these models, and also to
hear about other models for allowing anonymous postings.  You're
welcome to send messages to me directly if you don't want to bother
the whole list.

Russ Mills
rmills@night.vtc.vsc.edu



