<A NAME="2e89jl$6iq@ghost.McRCIM.McGill.EDU">  McGill Libraries decide not to "respect" publication ban</a><pre>
Newsgroups: alt.censorship,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,comp.org.eff.talk,can.general,ont.general,tor.general,can.legal,can.politics,soc.culture.canada,alt.journalism,alt.fan.karla-homolka
Subject:  McGill Libraries decide not to "respect" publication ban
Date: 9 Dec 1993 17:41:57 -0500
Message-ID: <2e89jl$6iq@ghost.McRCIM.McGill.EDU>

Summary:

	McGill Libraries have decided not to "respect" the publication
	ban, despite public requests from the Ontario Attorney General.


Details:

---------------------------------
A News Release dated December 2, 1993
from the Ontario Ministry of the Attorney General is entitled:

	"Importance of respect for publication ban
	stressed by Crown, Police, and Victim's Families"

	...
	The Ministry expresses its appreciation to newspaper distributors,
	cable companies, computer news services, libraries, and media outlets
						 *********
	who have indicated that they will respect the ban and not publish
	any material or provide access to any material which would
		     *********************************
	violate the ban.

---------------------------------

In a decision dated December 3, and now made public, McGill legal counsel
recommends newspapers, foreign and domestic, be made available as usual,
despite the fact that they are known to contain articles that violate the ban.
I personally checked in the McLennan periodical section today
and found examples that clearly violate the ban.

From the McGill legal opinion:

	"... merely placing the newspapers on the shelves of the periodicals
	room would not constitute a prohibited act."
	
	"... I certainly would feel quite comfortable defending the
	University against any complaint of criminal wrondoing in such
	a case.  Our defense is augmented by the fact that it is
	virtually impossible for the University to monitor the content
	of each and every periodical and newspaper it receives each day."

------------------------------------

Some Libraries around Canada are withdrawing from their shelves,
certain issues of Canadian and American newspapers that "may" violate
the ban.  Other libraries are asking staff to take scissors in hand
and remove specific articles, leaving little gaping rectangles.
I have heard on the news of specific examples
in Halifax, Ontario (various places), and Vancouver.


McGill's stance is "interesting" given its previous stance on
censoring electronic media.

</pre>
